from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("organ-systems-in-animals","After lunch Raju asked, \"Where does my food go?\" His teacher drew a long tube from the mouth to the anus.",
 ["Digestion is the breakdown of large, insoluble food molecules into small, soluble, diffusible ones by digestive enzymes. The alimentary canal is the passage from the mouth to the anus. Teeth are of different types, and the dental formula shows how many of each type are in one half of each jaw.",
  "The small intestine is the longest part of the canal, about 5 to 7 metres, and has the duodenum, jejunum and ileum, where digested food is absorbed. The liver and pancreas pour their juices into it to help digestion.",
  "The excretory system removes waste from the body, with the kidneys filtering the blood to make urine. The reproductive system, which is different in males and females, produces the new generation."],
 ["digestion","alimentary canal","dental formula","small intestine","kidney","reproductive system"],
 ("Which is the longest part of the small intestine?",["The small intestine has three parts.","They are duodenum, jejunum and ileum.","The ileum is the longest part."]),
 ("Absorption of digested food mainly happens in the stomach.",["Most absorption happens in the small intestine.","Its long coiled shape gives a large surface."]),
 ["Digestion makes food small and soluble.","The alimentary canal runs from mouth to anus.","The small intestine absorbs food.","Kidneys remove wastes."],
 ["m|The passage from mouth to anus is the|alimentary canal|trachea|aorta|Food travels in it.","t|Digestion needs enzymes.|T|Yes.","m|The longest part of the small intestine is the|ileum|duodenum|colon|About 5 to 7 m in all.","t|Kidneys form part of the digestive system.|F|They are part of excretion.","m|The dental formula shows|teeth in half a jaw|kinds of bone|blood groups|For upper and lower jaws.","s|Which organ filters blood to make urine?|kidney|Kidneys.","m|Digested food is absorbed mainly in the|small intestine|mouth|oesophagus|It has villi.","t|The reproductive system makes the new generation.|T|Yes."])
C("nutrition-and-health","Asha's lunch box had rice, dal, vegetables and a fruit. \"A balanced meal,\" said her mother.",
 ["Food is any substance of plant or animal origin eaten to give nutritional support. The classes of nutrients are carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals and water. Vitamin D is called the sunshine vitamin because our skin makes it in sunlight.",
  "Food hygiene means keeping food and surroundings clean so that it does not carry disease. Food preservation prevents spoilage so that food can be used later, by drying, heating, cooling, adding natural preservatives or irradiation, which exposes food to rays that kill germs and pests.",
  "Food adulteration is the mixing of cheap or harmful substances with food. Food quality control, with standards and tests, keeps food safe."],
 ["nutrients","vitamin D","food hygiene","preservation","irradiation","adulteration"],
 ("Name two methods of food preservation.",["Preservation prevents spoilage.","Drying removes water.","Irradiation kills germs; so these are two methods."]),
 ("Adulterated food is always safe.",["Adulteration mixes in cheap or harmful substances.","It can make food unsafe."]),
 ["A balanced diet has all nutrients.","Vitamin D comes from sunlight.","Preservation prevents spoilage.","Adulteration is harmful."],
 ["m|Vitamin D is called the|sunshine vitamin|bone vitamin|fruit vitamin|Skin makes it in sunlight.","t|Drying preserves food by removing water.|T|Yes.","m|Mixing cheap substances in food is|adulteration|irradiation|digestion|It is illegal.","t|Water is not a nutrient.|F|It is essential.","m|Exposure of food to rays to kill germs is|irradiation|distillation|filtration|Using gamma rays or UV.","s|Name the class of nutrients that builds the body.|proteins;protein|Proteins.","m|Clean handling of food is|food hygiene|adulteration|spoilage|It prevents disease.","t|Heating and cooling can preserve milk.|T|Pasteurisation."])
C("world-of-microbes","Meena saw a green fuzz on stale bread. \"Tiny living things grew there,\" her teacher said.",
 ["Microbes are tiny organisms seen only under a microscope. The main groups are bacteria, viruses, fungi and prions. A fungus has a body called a thallus, which is a mycelium in multicellular forms. Fungi living on living hosts are parasites and those on dead matter are saprophytes.",
  "Microbes are useful. Biofertilisers enrich the soil, microbes are used in industries such as making curd and bread, and in medicine such as antibiotics. They also cause diseases. The gap between infection and the first sign of disease is the incubation period.",
  "Airborne diseases spread through the air, waterborne through unclean water, and vector-borne diseases such as malaria, dengue, chikungunya and filaria through mosquitoes. AIDS and hepatitis-B are among the diseases spread by other routes. Immunisation with vaccines gives protection."],
 ["bacteria","virus","fungi","biofertiliser","incubation period","immunisation"],
 ("Which insect spreads malaria?",["Malaria is a vector-borne disease.","The vector is a mosquito.","The female Anopheles mosquito spreads it."]),
 ("All microbes cause disease.",["Many microbes are useful.","They make curd, biofertilisers and medicines."]),
 ["Microbes are bacteria, viruses, fungi and more.","Many are useful.","Mosquitoes are vectors of disease.","Vaccines protect us."],
 ["m|The body of a fungus is a|thallus|nucleus|root|Called mycelium in many forms.","t|Saprophytes live on dead matter.|T|Yes.","m|Malaria is spread by|mosquitoes|houseflies|air|A vector-borne disease.","t|All microbes are harmful.|F|Many are useful.","m|The time between infection and first symptoms is the|incubation period|vaccine|vector|It varies by disease.","s|Microbes that enrich soil are called|biofertilisers;biofertilizers|Used in farming.","m|Vaccines give|immunity|rest|energy|They prepare the body.","t|Dengue is a vector-borne disease.|T|Spread by mosquitoes."])
C("economic-biology","Raju's uncle grew vegetables in a green house and raised fish in a pond. \"That is economic biology,\" he said.",
 ["Horticulture is the science of growing fruits, vegetables and flowers. Pomology is fruit farming, olericulture is growing vegetables, floriculture is cultivating flowering plants and landscape gardening makes gardens beautiful. Vegetable forcing grows vegetables in buildings, green houses or other artificial conditions.",
  "Manures such as animal manure, compost and green manure improve the soil, and biofertilisers add nutrients. Medicinal plants give drugs called secondary metabolites. Mushroom cultivation, hydroponics (growing plants in water), aeroponics and aquaponics are modern methods.",
  "Dairy farming raises cattle for milk, aquaculture raises water animals, pisciculture is fish culture, prawn culture is rearing prawns and vermiculture uses earthworms to make compost."],
 ["horticulture","manure","biofertiliser","hydroponics","pisciculture","vermiculture"],
 ("What is floriculture?",["Flori means flower.","It is the cultivation of flowering and ornamental plants.","They are grown for beauty or for floristry."]),
 ("Hydroponics needs soil.",["Hydroponics grows plants in water with nutrients.","No soil is used."]),
 ["Horticulture grows fruit, vegetables and flowers.","Manures enrich soil.","Hydroponics grows plants in water.","Aquaculture raises water animals."],
 ["m|Growing vegetables is called|olericulture|pomology|floriculture|Olera means vegetables.","t|Compost is a kind of manure.|T|Yes.","m|Growing plants in water is|hydroponics|aquaponics only|vermiculture|Nutrients are in the water.","t|Pisciculture is fish culture.|T|Yes.","m|Earthworm culture is|vermiculture|aquaculture|floriculture|Used for compost.","s|What is fruit farming called?|pomology|Pomology.","m|Drugs from medicinal plants are called|secondary metabolites|fertilisers|enzymes|Plant chemicals.","t|Dairy farming is raising cattle for milk.|T|Yes."])
C("environmental-science","Asha watched a pond dry up in summer and fill again in the rains. \"Water moves in cycles,\" she said.",
 ["The biosphere is the part of Earth where life exists. Matter moves in biogeochemical cycles. The water or hydrological cycle is the continuous movement of water on Earth, and transpiration puts water vapour into the air. In the nitrogen cycle, ammonification breaks down nitrogenous waste into ammonium compounds and nitrifying bacteria change them to nitrates in nitrification.",
  "An adaptation is a feature that helps an organism live in its surroundings. Hydrophytes live in water, xerophytes in dry places and mesophytes in moderate conditions. Animals also have adaptations, such as those of the earthworm.",
  "Water conservation saves water, as in farm ponds, and recycling treats wastewater for reuse. The IUCN, the International Union for Conservation of Nature, works for the protection of nature."],
 ["biosphere","water cycle","nitrogen cycle","adaptation","xerophyte","water conservation"],
 ("Name plants adapted to dry places.",["Plants adapted to water-short places are xerophytes.","Cactus is an example.","So they are xerophytes."]),
 ("Nitrogen cannot be reused.",["Nitrogen moves in a cycle.","Bacteria change it between forms so it can be reused."]),
 ["Matter moves in cycles.","Adaptations help survival.","Xerophytes live in dry places.","Conserve and recycle water."],
 ["m|Plants of dry places are|xerophytes|hydrophytes|mesophytes|They save water.","t|The water cycle is a continuous movement of water.|T|Yes.","m|Conversion of ammonium into nitrates is|nitrification|ammonification|transpiration|Done by bacteria.","t|Hydrophytes live in deserts.|F|They live in water.","m|A feature that helps survival is an|adaptation|accident|cycle|Shaped by the habitat.","s|What does IUCN protect?|nature|It is a nature conservation union.","m|Recycling wastewater helps|conserve water|waste water|heat water|Treated water is reused.","t|A farm pond stores water for farms.|T|Yes."])
